#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124262 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "706fbf71-9085-506e-8f45-d1edf5965be1" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "2ae53015-78d7-521e-8e53-edf656a0a906" :question/math-academy-id "e-5507" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=PDP^{-1}$, find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D=P^{-1}AP$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A$.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$, we find that the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_3=-2 \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad \\lambda_2=-9\n$$\n\nThe second column of $P$ corresponds to $\\lambda=-2$. Computing $A-\\lambda I$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - (-2)I &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -8\\end{bmatrix} - (-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + 2R_{1} \\\\\n&= R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-3)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1 = -x_2+2x_3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-x_{2} + 2x_{3} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nComparing this with the second column of $P$, we deduce that $x_2=0$. Now, setting $x_3=1$ and substituting our values for $x_2$ and $x_3$ into the general solution, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{2}{1}=2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124278 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c24109-b1e9-56e8-977a-a86d5896e70a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Diagonal Matrix Corresponding to a Given Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "9610299d-5fe4-5fc2-abbc-c7dcadd5164e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5893" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nIf the matrix $A$ above is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}4 - λ & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 - λ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}4 - λ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)((4 - λ)(4 - λ) - 0) &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)(λ - 4)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=1$ (simple root) and $\\lambda_2=4$ (double root).\n\nLet's now find the dimension of the eigenspace corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root $\\lambda_1 = 1$. Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace $V_{1}$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1 = 1$ is\n$\\text{dim}(V_{1}) = 1$.\n- Next, we consider the double root $\\lambda_2 = 4$. Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace $V_{4}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-4I$:\n$A - 4I|= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} - 4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + R_{1}; R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-1)R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $2$nd one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda_2 = 4$. So,\n$\\text{dim}(V_{4}) = 2$.\n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, since\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(V_{1}) + \\text{dim}(V_{4}) = 1+2 = 3. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nFinally, a corresponding diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124328 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8ca065c-acc8-5ef2-9662-02114a90e620" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "6cc93bd0-c52c-56cf-bac4-ffcd67151dce" :question/math-academy-id "e-8495" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=PDP^{-1}$, what is the matrix $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A=PDP^{-1}$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A$. So let's apply the diagonalization algorithm to $A$.\n\n**Step 1.** From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=4 \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad \\lambda_3=0\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A$.\n\n- Consider $\\lambda=4$. Then,\n$A - λI|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} - 4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n$M|= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-\\frac{1}{2})R_{1}; R_{3}:=R_{3} + \\frac{1}{2}R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_2+3x_3$. Hence, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} + 3x_{3} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\nAs a result, we have two linearly independent eigenvectors$\\qquad$ $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Consider $\\lambda=0$. Then,\n$A - 0I|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} - 0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking for nonzero solutions of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$, we obtain the eigenvector $\\qquad$ $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the invertible matrix $P$ using our eigenvectors, as follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8ca065c-acc8-5ef2-9662-02114a90e620" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84821ebf-9895-5058-9270-289b8205a05e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122843} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf78176-2df0-5680-9e5d-6c742f11748c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122844} {:answer/id #uuid "b070015e-40d3-5fa7-bb4d-c87d11d74687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122845} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc68d1a-70a3-55d6-bb6c-c4679a0bc7c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122846} {:answer/id #uuid "e984b7c2-1a1e-57d4-9617-f7cf0dd38393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84821ebf-9895-5058-9270-289b8205a05e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122843} :answer-field/id #uuid "8470b5b3-7aee-53e2-b998-f8b84fbaa98a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122842}] :question/id #uuid "9399d519-5cd1-5971-80a1-a13cdf8380ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-37304" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -9\\end{bmatrix}, D = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A = PDP^{-1}$, which of the following could be the matrix $P$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c24109-b1e9-56e8-977a-a86d5896e70a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Diagonal Matrix Corresponding to a Given Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2c37c0f-dc5b-508a-a514-1be068a80ea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123077} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ba1895-787d-503d-a71c-b1b1f5d1177c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123078} {:answer/id #uuid "be5961ec-148d-5ebd-a0c3-b983bbeb5233" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123079} {:answer/id #uuid "083b5596-6910-5fc2-8cb7-a0add37d297e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123080} {:answer/id #uuid "32c241d9-6fdd-56f2-a215-65aa0754bf2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7ba1895-787d-503d-a71c-b1b1f5d1177c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123078} :answer-field/id #uuid "97745c0a-96a6-5984-a6fd-28e222d779af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123076}] :question/id #uuid "e789a1f2-e505-5573-bd28-9b02324eb807" :question/math-academy-id "q-37647" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIf the matrix $A$ above is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8ca065c-acc8-5ef2-9662-02114a90e620" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a18f6c89-eaa2-5476-bf43-e24ec5331bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123089} {:answer/id #uuid "a532b62f-5b8f-5a32-bf35-c25ef7153a47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123090} {:answer/id #uuid "017381cb-c539-5104-8744-17507d10e27d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123091} {:answer/id #uuid "548b376d-df18-5b08-b071-f7a8f94c21c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123092} {:answer/id #uuid "56e1ab6f-5f13-5e29-a85a-ef1838b44b96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a532b62f-5b8f-5a32-bf35-c25ef7153a47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123090} :answer-field/id #uuid "e649a7d1-4630-563d-b534-3c10cf39628f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123088}] :question/id #uuid "42aee01a-a47c-5fcb-b8cf-70fa4a2625ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-37717" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that $A = PDP^{-1}$, which of the following could be matrices $D$ and $P$?"}]}
